WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.180
Imagine a mad scientist

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:07.140
who is trying to create an artificial being

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.380
indistinguishable from a human

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:20.100
and early in the development of this machine,

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.980
he asks you for help testing the mind.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.660
And so he confines the mind to a box.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.420
The only way it can interact with the world

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.660
is by reading input messages and responding

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.880
by printing output messages.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.220
And he presents you with the following challenge,

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.820
he shows you two identical boxes:

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.980
the other is connected to a human being

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.160
typing the output messages.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.780
And you are allowed to ask the boxes

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.360
any sequence of questions you can imagine.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.860
Do you think you could distinguish the machine

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.060
from the human?

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.940
What would you ask?

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.100
A triumph of mathematical and mechanical skill,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.020
is this great new automatic calculator

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.680
at Harvard University.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.140
Intricate problems in mathematics

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.920
put through the machine in coded form

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.000
on tape are accurately solved

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.620
in a minute fraction of the time

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.100
required for a human calculation.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.880
In the first half of the twentieth century

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.360
general purpose computers

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.300
were only just emerging.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.940
But speculations were swirling

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.120
about what would or

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.080
wouldn't be possible in the near future

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.140
with robots or intelligent machines.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.660
It was a time when many human tasks

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.480
were quickly being swallowed  up by machines.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.480
People feared powerful industrialists

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.740
would soon be supported by great machines.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.740
Machines which could do anything.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.800
Resulting in a class of poor labourers

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.740
who would simply toil away

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.380
to power the machines.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.280
In nineteen-fifty, Alan Turing wanted to ground

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.240
these speculations about machine intelligence

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.500
using a scientific approach

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.040
known as a Turing Test.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.300
Where a successful pass would mean

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.300
unequivocally that whatever is in the box

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.620
could be identified as intelligent.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:41.800
And to this day,

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.480
no machine has been able to accurately

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.900
mimic conversation over long periods of time.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.520
Historically, the concept of knowledge

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.120
and answering questions

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.340
have always been intertwined.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.100
Across many cultures, there is a

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.440
deep rooted belief

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.980
that the source of all knowledge

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.660
comes from a higher power.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.620
And one way to access knowledge

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.320
from a higher power is by posing questions

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.780
and waiting for a sign.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.820
In ancient Greece, citizens could take their

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.480
toughest questions: usually key decisions

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.320
in social and political life

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:23.960
to a medium

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.360
who would enter a special location

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.060
known as an oracle.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.280
For example the medium Pythia from Delphi

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.780
would enter the Sanctuary of Apollo

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.260
ask her question,

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.580
fall into a trance-like state,

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.480
and emerge with the answer.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.060
Turing referenced this history

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.940
with his imaginary oracle machine.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.340
Inside his oracle machine

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.000
would be access to an oracle database:

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:55.700
a database which contained

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.920
all answers to all questions.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:02.060
In one column there is a list of all possible sentences,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.000
think of this as our question column,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.440
and many of these sentences would be meaningless but

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.140
every question you can think of

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.560
is in this book.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.040
And in a second column: the corresponding answers.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.740
If a machine had this oracle database,

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.440
it would be able to answer any question.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.700
But could Turing's oracle machine

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.920
exist in the physical world?

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.260
Well the question column alone

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.420
would contain every possible

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.500
combinatoin of English words.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.700
Even if we limit ourselves to all possible

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.440
twelve word sequences,

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.580
that results in 10 to the power of 72

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.120
different sentences.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.400
Now even if you shrunk every letter

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.680
down to the size of an atom,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.960
you would still end up with a book

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.540
larger than the size of the visible universe.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.220
So the oracle database cannot exist.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.680
When we store knowledge this way,

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.260
by simply memorizing facts,

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:11.720
we call this declarative knowledge.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.200
For instance memorizing your multiplication tables

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.060
is declarative knowledge.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.200
Music boxes can be said to 'know' a song

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:22.700
declaratively.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.680
The music-roll declares each note.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.520
This works well with small numbers

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.500
or short songs, but as the numbers get larger

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.640
and songs get longer you, will eventually

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.120
run out of memory.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.680
So if a machine only knows declaratively,

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.220
meaning it memorizes everything,

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.080
then there is a very real physical limitation:

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:48.760
space.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.240
So if a machine is passing a Turing Test,

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.260
we know it can't simply be memorizing

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.100
all possible answers.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.140
It must be taking advantage of another

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:03.880
kind of knowledge.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.900
It must know how to know.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:09.880
Meaning it goes through a process

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:10.940
to get an answer.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.920
For example knowing the rules of multiplication

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.100
or knowing how to compose new music.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.960
And this kind of knowledge,

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.780
known as procedural knowledge,

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:26.140
is the focus of computer science.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.420
How do things become known?

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.180
(Joe Fowler)

